{
  "id": 414276770,
  "iid": 21306,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "epan: Fix relative time if the first frame with a TS isn't frame 1",
  "description": "Get rid of the odd hack where frame_data->frame_ref_num is 0 if\nthis is a reference frame and just set frame_data->frame_ref_num\nto the frame number used for relative timestamp comparisons in\nevery case, even if it's frame_data->num. That simplifies the\nlogic, and frame_data already has the ref_time bitfield for telling\nif this is a time reference frame.\n    \nNote that there's a slight distinction between \"this is a time\nreference frame\" and \"this is the frame used for relative time\nstamp comparisons.\" The first frame in a capture with a timestamp\nis the latter but not the former (unless specifically marked as\nsuch by the user.) That can also happen in the odd situation where\nthe user has marked a frame without a timestamp as a \"reference\ntime frame\" (the next frame with a timestamp becomes the frame\nused for time comparisons but is not technically a reference time\nframe itself.)\n\nIn case where, e.g., the first frame in a capture doesn't have a\ntimestamp but the second frame does, this causes the relative time\nsince first frame in the capture to be correctly shown as 0 for\nframe 2, the first with a timestamp, instead of being left blank.\n(Because now it is compared to itself, as typically done for the\nfirst frame in a capture, instead of to the first frame, which has\nno timestamp.)\n\nSee the file in #19896 for an example. Ping #20691.",
  "state": "merged",
